3. Critical Correction

Do not model this as:

SSG
→ UCOB
→ MAP
→ ACOB
→ Execute


The current evidence indicates that this is incorrect.

The working model contains two COBOL compilation paths.

ACOB path
COBOL
  ↓
ACOB
  ↓
Relocatable Element
  ↓
MAP
  ↓
Absolute Element
  ↓
XQT

UCOB path
COBOL
  ↓
UCOB
  ↓
Object Module
  ↓
Link / Load / Execution mechanism
  ↓
XQT


Therefore:

ACOB is currently understood as a COBOL compiler.
UCOB is currently understood as a COBOL compiler.
MAP belongs to the collection/linking lifecycle.
XQT is the execution boundary.

Do not assume MAP is mandatory after UCOB.

Do not assume ACOB executes COBOL.

4. Generation Time vs Runtime

This distinction is mandatory.

Generation-time behaviour

Examples:

*SET
*DEFINE
*PROCESS
*INCREMENT
SGS lookup
variable substitution
conditional generation
generated COBOL
generated ECL


These operations affect what source/runstream is generated.

They must not automatically become runtime C# or PowerShell variables.

Runtime behaviour

Examples:

OPEN
READ
MOVE
COMPUTE
WRITE
CLOSE


These belong to the executing business program.

Always ask:

Does this operation happen while SSG constructs the stream, or when the generated program executes?

5. # Statements

Federale source may contain statements such as:

#UCOB
#ACOB
#MAP
#XQT


Do not assume these are separate commands named #UCOB, #ACOB, etc.

The current manual-backed working model indicates that SSG can transform a leading:

#


into:

@


in generated output under the applicable SSG sign-character configuration.

Therefore:

#ACOB


may generate:

@ACOB


and:

#XQT


may generate:

@XQT


Do not implement this as an unconditional textual # → @ replacement.

Reproduce the actual SSG semantics.

6. File Assignment Model

Keep these concepts separate:

SSG-generated filename

logical/internal program filename

physical OS 2200 file/resource


The working runtime model is:

ASG
 ↓
Assigned file/resource
 ↓
USE
 ↓
Internal/logical filename
 ↓
XQT
 ↓
Executing program
 ↓
OPEN / READ / WRITE


Do not confuse resource assignment with business file I/O.

7. Migration Principle

Do not mechanically translate every OS 2200 command into a PowerShell function.

Instead determine the semantic responsibility.

For example:

@ASG
@USE
@MAP
@XQT


represent OS 2200 facilities.

The AMT implementation may reproduce those semantics through:

AMT libraries
Control Center
.NET runtime behaviour
PowerShell orchestration
generated C#

The target does not need to look syntactically like OS 2200.

It needs to behave equivalently where behaviour matters.

